. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H4 * 
. 
.         * We standardize responses to plh0308_v2, a question that asks whethe
> r respondents believe it is "Best if man and woman work the same amount so th
> ey can share the responsibility" to construct our dependent variable, std_sup
> port_gen_eq.  We take this to a good measure of support for gender equality, 
> where effects can be measured by gender (H4). If H4 is true and having daught
> ers leads to a larger increase in support for gender equality for male parent
> s, having daughters will induce male respondents to increase their scale scor
> e response to plh0308_v2 (i.e., answering closer to 7; agreeing or agreeing m
> ore strongly with the statement) moreso than female respondents (β_numDaughte
> rs_MALE>0). We estimate the parameter of interest, the coefficient on numDaug
> hters_MALE, from a linear OLS regression analysis with std_support_gen_eq as 
> dependent variable and the count variable num_daughters_MALE as independent v
> ariable, controlling for demographic characteristics, missing data, country o
> f origin, state of residence, total family size, and survey wave. We weight r
> esponses using the individual weight (phrf) provided by the SOEP.
. 
. 
.         reg std_support_gen_eq num_daughters num_daughters_MALE r_* mi_* i.co
> rigin i.syear i.hgnuts1 num_kids [aweight=phrf] if num_kids!=0, r
